KG42.register("rules-0e7158b3bc31aee7d0776069",{"id":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R008","paper":"31-coalition_formation","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R008","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Local energy product","process_stage":"bidding","market_meaning":"The energy supplied for state-1 P2P trading is surplus generation. Scope: State-1 P2P trading in the considered prosumer network. Condition: The prosumer is in state 1, without charging or discharging its battery for P2P trading. The state-2 energy product includes energy discharged from batteries for sale to peers and energy purchased from peers for battery charging. Scope: State-2 P2P trading in the considered prosumer network. Condition: The prosumer is in state 2 and uses its battery for trading.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function product.local_energy under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["product.local_energy"],"E":[],"omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"bidding","context_text":"State-1 P2P trading in the considered prosumer network. | State-2 P2P trading in the considered prosumer network.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E003","v141-b-g01-E009"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"x_E_sur","symbol":"E_{n,sur}(t)","meaning":"Energy surplus of prosumer n at time slot t; in state 1 this is the energy product offered to peers when positive.","kind":"operational","domain":"Time-indexed energy surplus quantity defined by Eq. (2); source gives no separate numerical bound for this surplus variable in the supplied definition.","unit":null,"indices":["n","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E003","v141-b-g01-E007","v141-b-g01-E009"]},{"variable_id":"x_E_dis","symbol":"E_{n,dis}(t)","meaning":"Energy discharged from prosumer n's battery at time slot t for its own/household operation before P2P market participation.","kind":"operational","domain":"Energy quantity; source defines it in the system model and uses it in the surplus/deficiency definitions. The state-1 P2P battery contribution is separately stated to be zero.","unit":null,"indices":["n","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E006","v141-b-g01-E007","v141-b-g01-E010"]},{"variable_id":"x_E_cha","symbol":"E_{n,cha}(t)","meaning":"Energy charged to prosumer n's battery at time slot t for its own operation before P2P market participation.","kind":"operational","domain":"Energy quantity; source defines it as part of demand and uses it in the surplus/deficiency definitions.","unit":null,"indices":["n","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E006","v141-b-g01-E007"]},{"variable_id":"x_e_c","symbol":"e_{n,c}(t)","meaning":"Energy charged by prosumer n's battery at time slot t; in state 2 it represents energy purchased from peers for battery charging.","kind":"operational","domain":"Energy quantity; source states e_{n,c}(t) \u003c= E_{chr,dis} and also bounds it in (7).","unit":null,"indices":["n","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E009","v141-b-g01-E011","v141-b-g01-E013"]},{"variable_id":"x_e_d","symbol":"e_{n,d}(t)","meaning":"Energy discharged by prosumer n's battery at time slot t; in state 2 it represents battery energy sold to peers.","kind":"operational","domain":"Energy quantity; source states e_{n,d}(t) \u003c= E_{chr,dis} and also bounds it in (7).","unit":null,"indices":["n","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E009","v141-b-g01-E012","v141-b-g01-E013"]}],"theta":[{"parameter_id":"theta_E_pv","symbol":"E_{n,pv}(t)","meaning":"Solar generation of prosumer n at time slot t, treated as an exogenous time-indexed input in the surplus definition.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["n","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E006","v141-b-g01-E007"]},{"parameter_id":"theta_E_hou","symbol":"E_{n,hou}(t)","meaning":"Household energy demand of prosumer n at time slot t, treated as an exogenous time-indexed input in the surplus definition.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["n","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E006","v141-b-g01-E007"]}],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R008-C001","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The energy supplied for state-1 P2P trading is surplus generation. Scope: State-1 P2P trading in the considered prosumer network. Condition: The prosumer is in state 1, without charging or discharging its battery for P2P trading.","expression":"E_{n,sur}(t) = (E_{n,pv}(t) + E_{n,dis}(t)) - (E_{n,hou}(t) + E_{n,cha}(t))","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["product.local_energy"],"variable_refs":["x_E_sur","x_E_dis","x_E_cha"],"parameter_refs":["theta_E_pv","theta_E_hou"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E003","v141-b-g01-E007"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R008-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The state-2 energy product includes energy discharged from batteries for sale to peers and energy purchased from peers for battery charging. Scope: State-2 P2P trading in the considered prosumer network. Condition: The prosumer is in state 2 and uses its battery for trading.","expression":"\\{e_{n,d}(t)\\ \\text{(battery energy discharged for sale to peers)},\\ e_{n,c}(t)\\ \\text{(energy purchased from peers for battery charging)}\\}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["product.local_energy"],"variable_refs":["x_e_d","x_e_c"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E009","v141-b-g01-E011","v141-b-g01-E012"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R008-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"A state-1 prosumer sells surplus generation only when its surplus energy is positive.","expression":"E_{n,sur}(t) > 0","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["product.local_energy"],"variable_refs":["x_E_sur"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E009"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E003","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p02-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"1. Introduction","anchor_text":"al- prosumers. In particular, we use the framework of a cooperative game to [30] to capture the inte","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"State 1 refers to the state of a prosumer, in which it does not charge or discharge its battery for participating in P2P trading. It only uses its surplus generation for participating in the market. State 2 of a prosumer, on the other hand, refers to the state, in which it is willing to charge or discharge its battery for buying energy from or selling energy to the P2P market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E009","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"2. System model","anchor_text":"a its battery in the energy trading market. Similarly, a state 1 prosumer in only buys energy, when ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table 1","image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We further consider that the prosumers participating in the P2P energy trading choose to be either of the two states at any given time slot t.\n(a) State 1 Prosumers in state 1 do not charge or discharge their batteries for trading purposes and only sell their surplus E_{n,sur}(t) to other prosumers in N\\{n\\} or to the grid when E_{n,sur}(t) = (E_{n,hou}(t) + E_{n,cha}(t)) - E_{n,pv}(t) > 0. In other words, a prosumer in state 1, which we denote as an element of set W, does not put its battery in the energy trading market. Similarly, a state 1 prosumer only buys energy, when necessary, to meet the deficient energy E_{n,def}(t) = (E_{n,pv}(t) + E_{n,dis}(t)) - E_{n,hou}(t). Note that a prosumer without any battery always remains in state 1.\n(b) State 2 Prosumers is state 2 use their batteries for trading purposes. That is, a prosumer in state 2, which we indicate via V is motivated, e.g., for more economical benefit, to discharge its battery for selling energy to other prosumers. Similarly, it may also buy energy from other peers to charge its battery for future needs.\nNote that when a prosumer does not have a battery (prosumer 6) or it does not want to charge or discharge despite having battery (prosumer 5 in time slot 1) will remain in state 1."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E006","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p03-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"2. System model","anchor_text":"Applied Energy 261 (2020) 114436 u- that prosumer n needs for its household activities and En,cha t(","equation_id":"1-2","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"We assume that at any time slot t of the day, the generation of solar energy from prosumer n’s solar panel is E_{n,pv}(t), the amount of energy discharged from its battery is E_{n,dis}(t), and the energy demand of prosumer n is E_{n,dem}(t) = E_{n,hou}(t) + E_{n,cha}(t). Here, E_{n,hou}(t) is the energy that prosumer n needs for its household activities and E_{n,cha}(t) is the energy to charge the battery. Since the energy from the solar is free and from the battery is reasonably cheap, in reality the owner of the house prefers to use the solar and battery energy to meet the demand. Therefore, the amount of solar and battery self consumption by each house is\nE_{n,c}(t) = \\min(E_{n,pv}(t) + E_{n,dis}(t), E_{n,dem}(t)). (1)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E007","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p03-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"2. System model","anchor_text":"cordingly, d n,sur ( t ) = ( En ,pv (t ) + En,dis ( t )) ( En,hou ( t ) + En ,cha ( t )), (2) u- E a","equation_id":"2-3","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Accordingly, based on the generation, demand, and consumption of energy, each prosumer n may need to buy excess energy to meet its energy deficiency E_{n,def}(t) or has surplus energy E_{n,sur}(t) to sell. Accordingly,\nE_{n,sur}(t) = (E_{n,pv}(t) + E_{n,dis}(t)) - (E_{n,hou}(t) + E_{n,cha}(t)), (2)\nand\nE_{n,def}(t) = (E_{n,hou}(t) + E_{n,cha}(t)) - (E_{n,pv}(t) + E_{n,dis}(t)). (3)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E010","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.1 Utility of prosumer at state 1","anchor_text":"battery energy across different entities. Hence, from the perspective of to P2P trading, each prosum","equation_id":"4","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For prosumer at state 1 the contribution of a prosumer’s battery to the energy trading can be assumed to be 0 when considering the flow of battery energy across different entities. Hence, from the perspective of P2P trading, each prosumer n at state 1 can be considered as a prosumer with the grid-tie solar system without battery [43]. Therefore, prosumers of this type trade their solar energy either with one another prosumer or with the grid without charging or discharging their batteries. In this context, if the mid-market buying and selling prices per unit of energy are p_b(t) and p_s(t) respectively (following [39]), the utility and cost to a prosumer n can be defined as\nU_{n,s}(t) = p_s(t)E_{n,sur}(t), and J_{n,b}(t) = p_b(t)E_{n,def}(t) (4)\nrespectively for selling and buying their surplus and deficient energy."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E011","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.2 Utility of a prosumer at state 2","anchor_text":"thefact that the benefit to a prosumer for charging its battery even- wpr tually gets saturated with","equation_id":"5","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"We note that the utility that a prosumer obtains from charging its battery can be defined as the sum of the relevant cost and the benefit of charging. Benefit of charging can be captured by using a utility function with decreasing marginal benefit [34,44,45]. Such a property refers to the fact that the benefit to a prosumer for charging its battery eventually gets saturated with charging as the SoC s_n(t) of the battery becomes close to its available capacity b_{n,c}(t) = b_n - s_n(t).\nTo this end, we consider the utility of a state 2 prosumer n for charging its battery as:\nU_{n,c}(t) = k\\left[b_{n,c}(t)e_{n,c}(t) - \\frac{1}{2}\\alpha_n e_{n,c}(t)^2\\right] - (p_l + p_c(t))e_{n,c}(t). (5)\nIn (5), the term \\left(b_{n,c}(t)e_{n,c}(t) - \\frac{1}{2}\\alpha_n e_{n,c}(t)^2\\right) refers to the benefit of charging, 0 \u003c k \\le 1 is a scaling factor, and (p_l + p_c(t))e_{n,c}(t) is the cost of charging. Here, p_l is the degradation cost per kWh of the battery [46], p_c(t) is the price per unit of charging energy e_{n,c}(t) \\le E_{chr,dis} (where, E_{chr,dis} is the rated charging/discharging rate of the battery), and \\alpha_n > 0 is the satisfaction parameter of prosumer n [44]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E012","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.2 Utility of a prosumer at state 2","anchor_text":"states by prosumer 1 and 2 from timeslot 1 to 2 Prosumer 3, 4, 5 and 6 remain in the same state in b","equation_id":"6-7","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In the similar way, the utility of a state 2 prosumer n for discharging its battery can be defined as\nU_{n,d}(t) = k\\left[s_n(t)e_{n,d}(t) - \\frac{1}{2}\\alpha_n e_{n,d}(t)^2\\right] + (p_d(t) - p_l)e_{n,d}(t), (6)\nwhere p_d(t) is the price per unit of discharged energy e_{n,d}(t) \\le E_{chr,dis}. However, unlike (5), (p_d(t) - p_l)e_{n,d}(t) in (6) refers to the revenue that an n obtains from discharging its battery. Further,"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E013","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.2 Utility of a prosumer at state 2","anchor_text":"states by prosumer 1 and 2 from timeslot 1 to 2 Prosumer 3, 4, 5 and 6 remain in the same state in b","equation_id":"6-7","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"e_{n,c}(t) \\le \\min(e_{\\max}(t), b_{n,c}(t), E_{n,sur}(t)), \\quad e_{n,d}(t) \\le \\min(e_{\\max}(t), s_n, E_{n,def}(t)) (7)\nto ensure that battery charges or discharges neither more than its rated charging and discharging rate nor more than the network limit on the energy transfer. In other words,"}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":"C explicitly supplied only source-backed unary facts for this function; other graph gaps remain reviewable.","empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":null},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-participation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"product.local_energy","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E003","quote":"State 1 refers to the state of a prosumer, in which it does not charge or discharge its battery for participating in P2P trading. It only uses its surplus generation for participating in the market."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E009","quote":"a prosumer in state 2, which we indicate via V is motivated, e.g., for more economical benefit, to discharge its battery for selling energy to other prosumers. Similarly, it may also buy energy from other peers to charge its battery for future needs."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"469463021e8040ce324ac3a06274218104b1283c50891f77a6d7d3841d22435e","applicability":"formalized","reason":"Partially formalized with source-named quantities only. C001 is represented using the paper's general surplus definition (2) and C002 as the two state-2 battery-energy components named in the source; no netting or aggregate state-2 product scalar is invented. C003 transcribes the unambiguous state-1 positivity condition. The state-1-specific surplus line in B evidence v141-b-g01-E009 conflicts with Eq. (2) in v141-b-g01-E007, so that conflict is retained as a revision request rather than silently corrected. All B evidence remains unreviewed candidate support; no independent source audit is claimed.","bindings":[{"object_id":"x_E_sur","node_ids":["product.local_energy"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"E_{n,sur}(t) is the source-named surplus energy that constitutes the state-1 local energy product; the frozen rule has no finer-grained quantity node or edge for it."},{"object_id":"x_E_dis","node_ids":["product.local_energy"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"E_{n,dis}(t) enters the source surplus definition that defines the state-1 energy product; it is bound to the sole product.local_energy node."},{"object_id":"x_E_cha","node_ids":["product.local_energy"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"E_{n,cha}(t) enters the source surplus definition that defines the state-1 energy product; it is bound to the sole product.local_energy node."},{"object_id":"x_e_c","node_ids":["product.local_energy"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"e_{n,c}(t) is the source-named battery charging energy purchased from peers in state 2 and is a component of the local energy product."},{"object_id":"x_e_d","node_ids":["product.local_energy"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"e_{n,d}(t) is the source-named battery discharge energy sold to peers in state 2 and is a component of the local energy product."},{"object_id":"theta_E_pv","node_ids":["product.local_energy"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"E_{n,pv}(t) is an exogenous input in the source definition of surplus energy, which is the state-1 local energy product."},{"object_id":"theta_E_hou","node_ids":["product.local_energy"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"E_{n,hou}(t) is an exogenous input in the source definition of surplus energy, which is the state-1 local energy product."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R008-C001","node_ids":["product.local_energy"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The formal surplus relation defines the state-1 local energy product; it is attached to the only frozen node for this rule."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R008-C002","node_ids":["product.local_energy"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The component enumeration defines what the state-2 local energy product includes; no separate state-2 product node or edge exists in the frozen rule."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R008-C003","node_ids":["product.local_energy"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The positivity condition constrains when the state-1 surplus product is sold, so it is attached to product.local_energy."}],"revision_requests":["Resolve the printed state-1 surplus expression in B evidence v141-b-g01-E009, which states E_{n,sur}(t)=(E_{n,hou}(t)+E_{n,cha}(t))-E_{n,pv}(t)>0, against Eq. (2) in v141-b-g01-E007, which states E_{n,sur}(t)=(E_{n,pv}(t)+E_{n,dis}(t))-(E_{n,hou}(t)+E_{n,cha}(t)). C.1/B should confirm whether the E009 line is a transcription/printing error and whether E_{n,dis}(t) belongs in the state-1 surplus expression.","Clarify whether the state-2 local energy product should remain represented as the unordered source-named component pair {e_{n,d}(t), e_{n,c}(t)} or whether C.1 intends a scalar/net state-2 product. The supplied source text gives the two components but no single state-2 product equation.","Confirm the optimization roles of E_{n,dis}(t), E_{n,cha}(t), e_{n,d}(t), and e_{n,c}(t) for this bidding-stage rule; the source defines these as battery energy quantities but does not explicitly state which are decision variables at the local-energy-product stage versus prior or exogenous quantities."]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
